    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nFiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts **1 July**, labelled by the calendar year it ends \u2014 the Australian convention, matching this app's AU domain), with a single shared definition so all date-derived values agree.\n\n**New helper** \u2014 `rest/function/owner/FiscalYear.java`:\n- `of(date)` \u2192 fiscal-year number (`month \u2265 July ? year+1 : year`)\n- `label(date)` \u2192 `FY<YY>` (last two digits)\n\n**`fiscalYear` field (new)** \u2014 added to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml` (read-only `FY<YY>`) and mapped in `OwnerMapper` from the business-day-adjusted `registrationDate`. This is what the new acceptance test reads back.\n\n**Membership identifier's year segment (changed behaviour)** \u2014 `AssignOwnerMembershipNumber` now derives its `-M<YY>` from `FiscalYear.of(registrationDate)` instead of the plain calendar year.\n\n**Tenure (changed behaviour)** \u2014 `OwnerMapper.membershipPoints` now grants its +3 when at least one fiscal-year boundary has elapsed (`FiscalYear.of(now) \u2212 FiscalYear.of(registrationDate) \u2265 1`) rather than using a 365-day window. A newly created owner still has zero tenure (registers within the current fiscal year), preserving that documented invariant.\n\nSince the stored `registrationDate` is already rolled forward by `DefaultOwnerRegistrationDate`, all three values derive from the **business-day-adjusted** date as required. Descriptions in `openapi.yml` and the relevant Javadoc were updated to match.\n\n## Verification\nFull suite green \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` / `BUILD SUCCESS`. The run confirmed internal consistency: an owner registered 2026-08-14 (August \u2265 July) got `membershipNumber=\u2026-M27` and `fiscalYear` `FY27`.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec says tenure is now \"elapsed fiscal years\" but doesn't pin the exact threshold (and no test constrains it). I mapped the old \"exceeds 365 days\" (\u2248 one year elapsed) to \"\u2265 1 elapsed fiscal year\" \u2014 i.e. at least one 1-July boundary crossed. If you intended a stricter `> 1`, that's a one-line change.",
